## Actual Behavior

TODO: Describe actual behavior

Reported 2026-08-30 by session tm-tournament-station-f7 from the tm-tournament-station tree, verified against `src/` before filing.

## Defect

A read written in AngelScript property syntax is not credited to the accessor
that implements it, so the accessor is reported `a function 'get_X' is never
used` even where it is read from production files.

Two distinct forms, and they must BOTH be covered — fixing only the first
leaves the second live:

1. **Namespace-qualified**: `Directives::Showing` read in `Main.as` does not
   mark `Directives::get_Showing` used.
2. **Member on an instance**: `c.IsSecure` where the class declares
   `bool get_IsSecure()`.

## Confirmed instances (reporter's tree, all read outside test/dev files)

| accessor | read from |
|---|---|
| `Directives::get_Showing` | Main.as, Messages.as |
| `Directives::get_HistoryCount` | Main.as |
| `Messages::get_PendingCount` | Main.as |
| `Session::get_IsConnected` | Main.as, Exports.as |
| `Session::get_Status` | Main.as |
| `Session::get_TournamentName` | Main.as, Branding.as, Directives.as |

Not a threshold effect: `Directives::Showing` has 13 reads and is still
reported unused.

## Why (coordinator, pre-dispatch)

The machinery EXISTS and some path is not reaching it. `src/analysis/unused.rs`
contains no `get_`/`set_` handling at all (1303 lines, zero matches), but
`src/typecheck/checker.rs` has both:

- `record_value_use_variants` (:802) records `name`, `get_name`, `set_name`
- `record_member_use_from_type` (:816) records `Type::member`,
  `Type::get_member`, `Type::set_member` across the whole inheritance chain

So this is an INCONSISTENCY, not an unimplemented feature — which means the
fix is to route the missing paths through the existing helpers, not to invent
a new mechanism. Find why a namespace-qualified read does not reach
`record_value_use_variants`, and why the member form does not reach
`record_member_use_from_type` (receiver type unresolved? class-name
normalization failing? a different code path entirely?). Do not add a third
recorder.

Note the deliberate comment on `record_member_use_from_type`: it over-marks
rather than under-marks, because "a false 'unused' is far worse than a missed
warning". That principle decides any ambiguity in this ticket.

## Test plan (TDD, red first)

Red: a namespace `N` with `bool get_P()`, read as `N::P` from another file ->
currently reports `get_P` unused.

Variants (per CLAUDE.md, generalize — the reporter's two forms are the
minimum, not the set):
- namespace-qualified READ `N::P` -> marks `get_P`
- namespace-qualified WRITE `N::P = x` -> marks `set_P`
- read-modify-write `N::P += 1` -> marks BOTH
- member on instance `obj.P` -> marks `Type::get_P`
- member write `obj.P = x` -> marks `Type::set_P`
- through a handle `objHandle.P`
- inherited accessor declared on a PARENT class
- accessor declared in a dep/export, read from the dependent plugin
- getter-only property written to -> still an error, this must not silence it
- a genuinely unused `get_P` (no reads anywhere) -> STILL reported. This is the
  gate against over-marking everything to make the FP go away.
- `this.P` inside the declaring class (already works per :1196 docstring —
  pin it so the fix does not regress it)

## Gate

FP fixture ERROR/WARNING counts must not RISE. A DROP is expected here and is
the win. Measure two binaries on the same machine at the same time with
absolute paths, both plain and json.

## Related

Reporter's own correction, worth recording: they nearly filed ~19 of 24
warnings as false positives, and on checking found only 6 were. The other 13
were production functions whose only callers live in `*_Test.as`/`*_Dev.as`,
which a release build excludes — genuinely unused, and their own code smell.
Do not widen this ticket into 'unused analysis is too aggressive'. It is
specifically the property-syntax/accessor mapping.

## Discriminating gate (reporter, 2026-08-30) — USE THIS, it is the whole test

The reporting tree contains BOTH halves already classified, which makes it a
ready-made discriminator rather than just a source of examples. Repo:
`~/src/openplanet/my-plugins/tm-tournament-station` at master; `just lsp`
reproduces exactly 24 warnings; the breakdown is in its `AGENTS.md`.

- **6 must DISAPPEAR** — the table above, including `c.IsSecure` (member form).
- **18 must SURVIVE** — 13 production functions whose only callers live in
  `*_Test.as`/`*_Dev.as` (excluded from a release build, so genuinely unused),
  4 export-surface functions that are B062's business and NOT this ticket's,
  and 1 with no caller anywhere (`Branding::get_Logo`).

**The part that makes it a real gate: 8 of those 13 are THEMSELVES `get_`
accessors** — `Directives::get_Latest`, `get_PendingCount`, `get_ShownAt`,
`get_HasSnoozed`, `Messages::get_Showing`, `MatchGuard::get_Current`,
`get_MsInCurrent`, `Transport::get_IsSecure`.

So the lazy fix — stop warning on accessors, or mark every `get_X` used
whenever `X` appears anywhere — PASSES the six-row table and silently eats
eight correct warnings in the same repo. Same-shaped symbol, opposite expected
outcome, one tree. A fix is only correct if it moves 24 -> 18, not 24 -> 10.

Note `Transport::get_IsSecure` appears in BOTH lists: read as `c.IsSecure` from
production (must disappear) while a different `Transport` accessor is test-only
(must survive). Confirm which is which against the tree before asserting
either — do not assume the name alone decides it.

## Lane decision (coordinator, 2026-08-30)

Serialised behind B060 in lane `b060-b062-property-semantics`; B062 follows.
Shared files: `src/typecheck/checker.rs` with B060, `src/analysis/unused.rs`
with B062. **Blocked until B059 merges** — lane-b059 is live in `checker.rs`.

See B060 for the framing: it and this ticket are the same missing concept from
opposite sides. B060 applies the `X` <-> `get_X` mapping where it must not (a
property read classified as function-shaped, killing overload resolution);
this ticket fails to apply it where it must (a property read not crediting its
accessor as used). Solving B060 first may hand you the predicate this needs.

## The shared predicate does NOT close both (measured by lane-b060-b062, 2026-08-31)

The framing appended to B060 asked whether one "this expression is a property
read" predicate fixes B060 and B061 together. **Answer: no**, established by
measurement, not by reading.

Direct evidence: with B060's fix in (`a1e8f43`), the fixture still reports
`Ns::get_Slot`, `Ns::get_Handler` and `VNs::get_PtrP` as never used while
reading them as `Ns::Slot` / `Ns::Handler` / `VNs::PtrP` in the same files —
warning output byte-identical to master, 6 warnings both sides.

Two structural reasons, both worth keeping:

1. **Opposite polarity.** B060 asks about an *expression* ("does this syntax
   denote a function?"), answered before any name is recorded, and its fix is
   to STOP widening `X` -> `get_X`. B061 asks about a *name* ("which
   declarations does this read credit?") and its fix is to ENSURE widening
   happens. A shared helper would have to be consulted with opposite polarity
   at each site — that is the two fixes plus indirection.
2. **Different pipeline stages.** B060's fix is on the typed-expression path
   (it needs `arg_ty`). B061's failure is that the namespace-qualified read
   never reaches a recorder at all.

So B061 keeps its own fix and its own red test. Do not refactor toward a
shared predicate on the strength of the shared vocabulary.

## CORRECTION to this ticket's gate (lane-b060-b062, 2026-08-31; accepted)

**`Transport::get_IsSecure` belongs in the 18 that SURVIVE, not the 6 that go.**
The ticket lists `c.IsSecure` among the six that must disappear, while itself
flagging Transport as appearing in both lists and saying to confirm against the
tree. Confirmed: the tree holds exactly four `IsSecure` reads and **all four
are in `src/Transport_Test.as`**. There is no production read, so by this
ticket's own rule — test-only callers mean genuinely unused — it survives.

**Consequence, and it narrows the ticket.** The second claimed failure mode,
"member on an instance", rested entirely on that one example. Probing shows the
member form already credits its accessor correctly. So **B061 is a single-form
bug: namespace-qualified reads only.** That is consistent with all six rows in
the ticket's own table being namespace-qualified (`Directives::Showing`,
`Directives::HistoryCount`, `Messages::PendingCount`, `Session::IsConnected`,
`Session::Status`, `Session::TournamentName`).

**Likely root cause (by inspection, confirm by measurement before fixing):**
`record_value_use_variants` builds variants as `format!("get_{}", name)`. For a
qualified name that yields **`get_Ns::Slot`**, which can never match anything —
the correct form is `Ns::get_Slot`. `global_scope.rs::qualified_virtual_name`
already does this correctly. Route the missing path through that existing
helper; do NOT add a third recorder.

**The gate that actually discriminates:** `Directives::PendingCount` must
survive while `Messages::PendingCount` goes, and `Messages::Showing` must
survive while `Directives::Showing` goes. Same short name, opposite outcome —
anything keyed on the bare name gets these backwards, and only the qualified
form can split them. Pin both directions of that pair explicitly.

**Baseline confirmed:** B060 leaves this ticket's gate at exactly 24
diagnostics on `tm-tournament-station` (24 on merge-base, 24 on B060's branch),
so B061's drop is attributable to B061 alone.

## Corrections from lane-b060-b062 (2026-08-31), measured

**Root cause confirmed by measurement, not inspection.** One fixture, four
predictions, all four confirmed:

| read form | accessor | result |
|---|---|---|
| `Ns::P` (qualified read) | `Ns::get_P` | reported unused - WRONG |
| `Ns::P = 5` (qualified write) | `Ns::set_P` | reported unused - WRONG |
| `G` / `G = 7` (global) | `get_G` / `set_G` | credited correctly |
| `c.M` / `c.M = 9` (member) | `C::get_M` / `C::set_M` | credited correctly |

**The `set_` half is what makes this a discriminator rather than a plausible
story.** If the defect were "getters are not credited", the setter would
behave differently. It does not, because both go through the same
`format!("get_{}", name)` / `format!("set_{}", name)` prefix placement, which
for `Ns::P` produces **`get_Ns::P`** - a name nothing can ever match.
`qualified_virtual_name` in `global_scope.rs` already builds `Ns::get_P`
correctly, so the fix is to route through it.

Global and member forms both working confirms the single-form reading: this is
namespace-scoped resolution only.

## THE TICKET UNDERSTATES THE SURFACE: two spellings, not one

The forms list omits the second, and a test pinning only the first would pass
while half the defect survives:

1. **explicit `Ns::P`** from outside the namespace
2. **bare `P` resolved through the namespace ladder from inside
   `namespace Ns`** - observed live, where `Nz::get_NsShadowP` is read as bare
   `NsShadowP` and still reported unused

Both go through the same recorder, so one fix covers both. **Pin both anyway.**
One fix covering two spellings is not a reason to test one.

## FACTUAL ERROR IN THIS TICKET

All four `IsSecure` reads are in `Transport_Test.as`. They therefore belong in
the **18 that survive**, not the **6 that go**. Correct the counts before using
them as an acceptance criterion - a fix measured against the wrong expected
population would look wrong when it is right, or right when it is wrong.

## Coordinator ruling (2026-08-31): the acceptance criterion is 24 -> 16, NOT 24 -> 18

The lane was right and this ticket was wrong. **I verified both disputed rows
at source myself rather than accepting the report**, in
`~/src/openplanet/my-plugins/tm-tournament-station`:

    src/Branding.as:150   UI::Texture@ get_Logo() { return g_Logo; }
    src/Branding.as:155   void RenderMark(float height) { auto tex = Logo; ...

    src/MatchGuard.as:229 uint get_MsInCurrent() { ... }
    src/MatchGuard.as:238   if (requireSettled && MsInCurrent < SETTLE_MS) ...
    src/MatchGuard.as:329   ... IsSafeForDirectiveTimed(_lastCtx, ..., MsInCurrent);

`RenderMark`, `IsSafeToInterrupt` and `IsSafeForDirective` are production
functions, not tests. Both accessors have genuine bare property-syntax reads
inside their own namespace, so both MUST be credited. Hitting 18 would mean
declining to credit two real production reads — failing this ticket's purpose
in order to satisfy its arithmetic.

**Why the ticket was wrong, which is the part worth keeping.** The expected
population was assembled by searching for `get_X(` CALL syntax. That method
cannot, by construction, see a property-syntax read — which is precisely the
blindness this ticket exists to fix. **The acceptance criterion was derived
with the very method the ticket was filed to replace**, so it was guaranteed to
overcount the survivors by exactly the number of property-syntax reads, which
is the population under test.

This is the THIRD row misclassified the same way (`Transport::get_IsSecure` was
the first, corrected earlier). Three instances from one derivation is not three
mistakes; it is one broken method producing three outputs.

The ticket's own warning — "confirm against the tree, do not assume the name
alone decides it" — was correct and was simply not applied to these rows.

## Discriminating evidence, verified by the coordinator

The crossed pair splits on live code in BOTH directions, which is what proves
the fix is namespace-qualified rather than bare-name-keyed:

| short name | production reads | credited (goes) | survives |
|---|---|---|---|
| `Showing` | 3, all `Directives::` (`Main.as:399`, `Messages.as:66`, `Messages.as:93`) | `Directives::get_Showing` | `Messages::get_Showing` |
| `PendingCount` | 2, all `Messages::` (`Main.as:406`, `Main.as:408`) | `Messages::get_PendingCount` | `Directives::get_PendingCount` |

Same short name, opposite outcome, both ways. A bare-name-keyed fix gets one
wrong whichever way it guesses, so passing this pair is not something an
over-broad fix could do by luck.

Note the coordinator nearly misread this: an initial `head -6` on the `Showing`
reads returned only test files, which would have suggested no production read
existed. That was a truncation limit, not a finding. Full listing gives 3
production reads, all `Directives::`.

## The other half of the fix, which this ticket's forms list omits

Prefix placement was only half. `record_value_use_variants` built
`format!("get_{}", name)`, yielding `get_Ns::P` for `Ns::P` — a name nothing
can match. But a bare `P` inside `namespace Ns` records `get_P`, and `is_used`
DELIBERATELY refuses to trust a bare tail as a suffix match (it requires two
`::` segments), so it cannot reach `Ns::get_P` either. The recorder therefore
also qualifies by each enclosing namespace depth. Over-marking rather than
under-marking is this module's stated preference, and the boundary was measured
rather than assumed: a LOCAL named `P` inside `namespace N` credits
`N::get_P` on neither binary.